- 2026-11-11 (harness 9): I started the harness with a trailing `&` in a foreground call, so it was untracked; I stopped it by its exact command line and restarted it with run_in_background. Guard: long local runs start only with run_in_background, never with `&`.
- 2026-11-11 (launcher r12-r15): four review rounds each found new gaps in a self-made spend bound (rate file, unknown-price marker, lock). Guard: when a review loop keeps finding defects in one mechanism, replace the mechanism with an external record before patching again (r16: the account balance difference); ask "can this part be removed?" at the second rejection on the same mechanism.
- 2026-11-11 (harness 12): a macOS update restarted the Mac mid-run; the harness and its log in the /tmp scratchpad were lost (about 2 h). Guard: write long-run logs under results/pods/logs_harness/ (persists across restarts), and after any restart first check `runpod.py list` and `podctl.py balance` before relaunching.
- 2026-11-11 (harness 13): the harness ran under run_in_background, so it died with the Claude session when the session restarted (5 of 14 scenarios done, about 1.7 h lost). This replaces the harness-9 guard. Guard: long local runs start detached (`nohup ... < /dev/null &`, `disown`), write a pid file and the file hashes at the top of a log under results/pods/logs_harness/, and a separate tracked waiter (run_in_background) watches the pid and the log; after a session restart, check the pid before anything else.
- 2026-11-11 (reset results r1): I started the two reviewers with `set -- $seat` in the session shell, which is zsh; zsh does not split the words, so codex got no model and both seats failed at once. Guard: reviewers start only through `results/pods/review_start.sh TOPIC` (a bash script that refuses a missing brief or an existing review file).
